  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 1230 PM CDT Mon Oct 19 2026\n\nThe dry and above normal fall weather will continue through the\nbeginning of the weekend. By Wednesday, a trough moving into \nthe Intermountain West will amplify the upper ridge, which in \nturn will increase 1000-500 hPa thickness. As a result, we're \nexpecting a continued warming trend with highs in the 70s \nthrough at least the beginning of the weekend. Friday and \nSaturday we'll see the surface pressure gradient increase\nbetween the encroaching trough from the Intermountain West and\nthe departing Great Lakes ridge. This will induce gusty\nsoutherly winds across the region. Will have to keep an eye on\nthe potential for deeper mixing and warmer highs possibly in\nthe upper 70s/near 80 in some locations during this time, but \nfor now indications are we may have cloud cover to contend with\nmaking this lower confidence. In addition, increasing low level\nmoisture and RH values should mitigate any fire weather \nconcerns. However, if the winds were to stay slightly backed \nfrom SSE with some component of dry advection off the departing \nhigh then this could change, so it's worth keeping an eye on as\nwell.\n\nOver the weekend, precipitation chances may return, though there\nis considerable uncertainty in when, where, and to what degree \nbased on model spread of strength/tracking/timing. Most guidance\nsuggests any system would not reach the area until late in the\nweekend or early next week at the earliest. \n\nLastly, an additional look ahead suggests that while this late \nweekend system may only drop temperatures back to near normal, \nit will not end our stretch of above normal temperatures. CPC \nhighlights a very high combined probabilities (97%) for near \nnormal to above normal temperatures through the end of October, \nas seen in the 6-10 and 8-14 day outlooks.",
